{"id":"swim-in-rising-water","title":"Swim in Rising Water","difficulty":"Hard","pattern":"Advanced Graphs","tags":["Graph","Heap / Priority Queue","Binary Search","Union Find","Matrix"],"companies":{"google":2,"amazon":1},"description":"You are given an `n x n` grid where `grid[i][j]` is the elevation of that cell; the elevations are a permutation of `0..n*n-1`. At time `t` the water level is `t`, and you can stand on or move between 4-directionally adjacent cells whose elevation is **at most `t`**. Swimming takes no time.\n\nReturn the least time `t` at which you can travel from the top-left cell to the bottom-right cell.","examples":[{"input":"grid = [[0,2],[1,3]]","output":"3","explanation":"The destination has elevation 3, so no earlier time can work; at t = 3 every cell is reachable."},{"input":"grid = [[0,1,2,3,4],[24,23,22,21,5],[12,13,14,15,16],[11,17,18,19,20],[10,9,8,7,6]]","output":"16","explanation":"Follow the snake 0..16 along the border; the cells above 16 on the second row are never needed."}],"constraints":["1 <= n <= 50","0 <= grid[i][j] < n*n","All values are distinct."],"hints":["You need the path whose maximum elevation is as small as possible, not the shortest path.","Dijkstra with the heap keyed on 'highest elevation seen so far on this path' does exactly that: the first time you pop the target, its key is the answer.","Alternative: binary search on t with a BFS feasibility check, or union-find adding cells in increasing elevation until the corners connect."],"function":"swimInWater","signature":"def swimInWater(self, grid: List[List[int]]) -> int:","arg_types":{"grid":"List[List[int]]"},"return_type":"int","compare":"exact","tests":[{"input":{"grid":[[0,2],[1,3]]},"output":3},{"input":{"grid":[[0,1,2,3,4],[24,23,22,21,5],[12,13,14,15,16],[11,17,18,19,20],[10,9,8,7,6]]},"output":16},{"input":{"grid":[[0]]},"output":0},{"input":{"grid":[[3,2],[0,1]]},"output":3},{"input":{"grid":[[0,3,8],[7,4,5],[1,2,6]]},"output":6},{"input":{"grid":[[0,7,14,