02,309,316,323,330,337,344,351,358,365,372,379,386,393],[400,407,414,421,428,435,442,449,456,463,470,477,484,491,498,505,512,519,526,533,540,547,554,561,568,575,582,589,596,603,610,617,624,631,638,645,652,659,666,673,680,687,694,701,708,715,722,729,736,743],[750,757,764,771,778,785,792,799,806,813,820,827,834,841,848,855,862,869,876,883,890,897,904,911,918,925,932,939,946,953,960,967,974,981,988,995,1002,1009,1016,1023,1030,1037,1044,1051,1058,1065,1072,1079,1086,1093],[1100,1107,1114,1121,1128,1135,1142,1149,1156,1163,1170,1177,1184,1191,1198,1205,1212,1219,1226,1233,1240,1247,1254,1261,1268,1275,1282,1289,1296,1303,1310,1317,1324,1331,1338,1345,1352,1359,1366,1373,1380,1387,1394,1401,1408,1415,1422,1429,1436,1443],[1450,1457,1464,1471,1478,1485,1492,1499,1506,1513,1520,1527,1534,1541,1548,1555,1562,1569,1576,1583,1590,1597,1604,1611,1618,1625,1632,1639,1646,1653,1660,1667,1674,1681,1688,1695,1702,1709,1716,1723,1730,1737,1744,1751,1758,1765,1772,1779,1786,1793],[1800,1807,1814,1821,1828,1835,1842,1849,1856,1863,1870,1877,1884,1891,1898,1905,1912,1919,1926,1933,1940,1947,1954,1961,1968,1975,1982,1989,1996,2003,2010,2017,2024,2031,2038,2045,2052,2059,2066,2073,2080,2087,2094,2101,2108,2115,2122,2129,2136,2143],[2150,2157,2164,2171,2178,2185,2192,2199,2206,2213,2220,2227,2234,2241,2248,2255,2262,2269,2276,2283,2290,2297,2304,2311,2318,2325,2332,2339,2346,2353,2360,2367,2374,2381,2388,2395,2402,2409,2416,2423,2430,2437,2444,2451,2458,2465,2472,2479,2486,2493]]},"output":2493}],"solution":"class Solution:\n    def swimInWater(self, grid: List[List[int]]) -> int:\n        n = len(grid)\n        heap = [(grid[0][0], 0, 0)]  # (max elevation on path so far, r, c)\n        seen = {(0, 0)}\n        while heap:\n            t, r, c = heapq.heappop(heap)\n            if r == n - 1 and c == n - 1:\n                return t\n            for nr, nc in ((r + 1, c), (r - 1, c), (r, c + 1), (r, c - 1)):\n                if 0 <= nr < n and 0 <= nc < n and (nr, nc) not in seen:\n                    seen.add((nr, nc))\n                    heapq.heappush(heap, (max(t, grid[nr][nc]), nr, nc))\n        return -1\n","approach":"**Minimise the maximum elevation along the path (a bottleneck shortest path), which is Dijkstra with `max` in place of `+`.** Key the heap on the highest cell seen so far along the route. Popping a cell with key `t` means there is a route to it whose cells are all <= `t`, and because the heap is monotone no later pop can improve it. The first time the bottom-right corner is popped, `t` is the answer.\n\nBecause the key of a neighbour is `max(t, grid[nr][nc])` and never decreases, a cell can be marked visited the moment it is pushed; there is no need for a decrease-key.\n\n**Alternatives worth naming:** binary search on `t` with a BFS check, O(n^2 log n^2); union-find adding cells in increasing elevation and stopping when the two corners share a root, also O(n^2 log n^2) due to the sort.\n\n**Common mistakes:** treating it as a plain shortest path (summing elevations), forgetting that the starting cell itself can be the bottleneck, and allowing revisits which blows up the heap.\n\n**Interview follow-ups:** Compare the three approaches and their constants. How would you return the path? (Parent pointers at push time.) What if the water rose by more than one unit per second? (Return the first time step >= t.)","complexity":{"time":"O(n^2 log n)","space":"O(n^2)"},"similar":["network-delay-time","path-with-minimum-effort","min-cost-to-connect-all-points"],"number":119,"starter":"class Solution:\n    def swimInWater(self, grid: List[List[int]]) -> int:\n        pass\n"}